pecific polling, the emergence of clear nominees on each side, or a sharp shift in the national political environment could move this forecast.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Louisiana remains one of the more reliably Republican states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly eleven points toward the GOP. With the incumbent president term-limited, both parties will nominate fresh candidates, and neither side carries an incumbency advantage. No state-level general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market pricing are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's underlying partisan fundamentals.\n\nHeadlines so far center on the 2028 primary contests and on redistricting battles, including Democratic efforts targeting Louisiana's congressional map. Those fights could reshape House districts, but they do little to alter the statewide presidential picture. The Republican nominee is favored by a solid margin, though the absence of polling leaves meaningful uncertainty around the exact size of that edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.108, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.162, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1608, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1621, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1551, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.155, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1556, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1525, "margin": -11.0}]}